\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Projected Gaussian laws and the massless limit}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{For the [kernel decomposition](fdip-0031), let #{\nu_m} be the
projection onto #{H} of the law with precision #{L+m^2I}, #{m>0}.
Then #{\nu_m} converges to the [constrained law](fdip-000C) #{\nu_H}
in total variation, meaning
#{\sup_{A\text{ Borel}}|\nu_m(A)-\nu_H(A)|\to0}.
For every fixed polynomial #{P} on #{H}, its expectations converge too.}
\proof{
\p{By [kernel factorization](fdip-0033), the densities on #{H} are
#{f_m=w_m/Z_{H,m}}, where
##{\begin{aligned}
w_m(x)&=e^{-h(x,(L_H+m^2I_H)x)/2},\\
w_0(x)&=e^{-h(x,L_Hx)/2}.
\end{aligned}}
They are continuous, #{w_m\to w_0} pointwise, and
#{0\leq w_m\leq w_0}, with #{\int_H w_0=Z_H<\infty}.
Dominated convergence gives #{Z_{H,m}\to Z_H>0}.
For sufficiently small #{m}, #{Z_{H,m}\geq Z_H/2}; hence
##{|f_m-f_0|\leq 3w_0/Z_H.}
Another application gives #{\int_H|f_m-f_0|\to0}. For each Borel #{A},
#{|\nu_m(A)-\nu_H(A)|\leq\int_H|f_m-f_0|}, proving the uniform claim.
For a fixed polynomial, the dominating function
#{3|P|w_0/Z_H} is integrable by [finite polynomial moments](fdip-002W),
so #{\int P f_m\to\int P f_0}. Each use of dominated convergence
applies along every positive sequence #{m_j\to0}; the sequential
criterion gives the real-parameter limits as #{m\downarrow0}.
When #{H=0}, these laws are all the same point mass.}
}
\p{For a fixed source #{j\in E} and #{\ell_j(x)=h(j,x)}, the
[inverse-covariance formula](fdip-000B) and the kernel split give
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}_{Q_m}(\ell_j)
 &=m^{-2}\|P_Kj\|^2\\
 &\quad+\sum_{\lambda_a>0}
       \frac{h(j,u_a)^2}{\lambda_a+m^2}.
\end{aligned}}
Every term is nonnegative. The finite sum converges; the first term
is finite in the limit precisely when #{P_Kj=0}. Thus the variance
has a finite limit if and only if #{j\in H}, in which case it is
#{h(j,L^+j)}.}
\p{For a connected nonempty graph with positive edge weights, put #{n=|V|}.
The source #{u_0=\mathbf1_V/\sqrt n} has
variance #{m^{-2}} as a source. Its contribution to every Green-kernel
entry is #{1/(nm^2)}, and #{G_m(v,v)\geq 1/(nm^2)}. A field difference
has source #{\delta_v-\delta_w\in H}, so its variance converges to the
constrained variance. On a disconnected graph this holds for vertices
in the same component; a difference between components has a nonzero
kernel projection. These observable limits do not supply a normalized
full-space massless density.}
\p{The analytic ingredient is
\citet{Theorem 1.4.49, pp. 111-112, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
The three dominators above verify its hypotheses for these Gaussian
laws and their fixed polynomial observables.}
